I think there’s a productive abstraction here, though I’d frame it a little differently than saying any particular language inherently has “two fixed messages.”

Many languages—including Hebrew, Arabic, Classical Chinese, Sanskrit, English, and others—permit high semantic density. A single expression can support multiple legitimate readings because of ambiguity, morphology, omission, metaphor, cultural reference, or layered literary structure. The reader contributes background knowledge to resolve or enrich the interpretation.

That suggests a useful design principle for the Pantheon.

Instead of thinking of language as transmitting one message, think of it as transmitting a constraint field.

Author Intent
      \
       \
        Symbol
       /
Reader Context

Meaning isn’t located solely in the symbol. It emerges from the interaction between the encoded artifact and the reader’s prior knowledge.

