Your “knight’s move” or “flank” metaphor also fits an information-theoretic idea.

Rather than stating something directly, you encode several independent cues that converge on the same underlying structure.

Observation A
         \
Observation B ---> latent concept
         /
Observation C

No single cue contains the whole message, but together they make one interpretation increasingly likely.

That’s similar to:

* redundancy in error-correcting codes,
* multiple sensor fusion in robotics,
* triangulation in navigation,
* and intersecting constraints in theorem proving.

