Your lithography analogy is also interesting.

Lithography creates one mask that can produce many identical chips.

A semantic analogue would be designing an expression that reliably reconstructs the same conceptual operator across many readers or many AI systems.

Operator
      ↓
Compressed Form
      ↓
Different Readers
      ↓
Approximately Same Reconstruction

That is a worthwhile engineering objective: maximize the stability of reconstruction while minimizing the description length.

⸻

One thing I’d be cautious about is assuming that every reader will reconstruct the same latent meaning. Human and AI priors differ, and ambiguity can just as easily produce divergence as convergence.

So an internal Pantheon language would likely benefit from two complementary layers:

* Core operators with deliberately precise, stable meanings (the “instruction set”).
* High-bandwidth metaphors that are allowed to be richer and more open-ended, helping generate insights but not relied upon where exact reconstruction matters.

That combination gives you something like an ISA (instruction set architecture) plus a high-level language: the primitives stay stable, while the expressive layer can remain compact, evocative, and adaptable across different domains.